Q("C", 1, [P("At what range of substrate concentration will an enzyme with a k<sub>cat</sub> of 30 s<sup>−1</sup> and a K<sub>m</sub> of 0.005 M show one-quarter of its maximum rate?")],
  ["3.0 × 10<sup>−3</sup> M to 3.1 × 10<sup>−3</sup> M", "0.65 × 10<sup>−3</sup> M to 0.75 × 10<sup>−3</sup> M", "1.65 × 10<sup>−3</sup> M to 1.75 × 10<sup>−3</sup> M", "2.7 × 10<sup>−3</sup> M to 2.8 × 10<sup>−3</sup> M"],
  E("From Michaelis–Menten, v/V<sub>max</sub> = [S]/(K<sub>m</sub> + [S]) = 1/4, so 4[S] = K<sub>m</sub> + [S] and [S] = K<sub>m</sub>/3 = 0.005/3 = 1.67 × 10⁻³ M. k<sub>cat</sub> is a distractor: the fraction of V<sub>max</sub> depends only on [S]/K<sub>m</sub>.",
    {"1": "≈3 mM gives v ≈ 0.38 V<sub>max</sub>.", "2": "≈0.7 mM gives v ≈ 0.12 V<sub>max</sub>.", "4": "≈2.75 mM gives v ≈ 0.35 V<sub>max</sub>."})),
# C3
Q("C", 1, [P("A cytoplasmic monomeric protein containing a single non-surface exposed cysteine residue precipitates upon mutation of the Cys to 'Ile'. However, the mutation of Cys to 'Ala' leads to a soluble and functional protein equivalent to the native form. Which one of the following statements explains the above observations?")],
  ["Cys mutated to 'Ile' alters the net charge of the protein, while mutation to 'Ala' does not.", "Cys mutated to 'Ala' alters the net charge of the protein, while mutation to 'Ile' does not.", "Cys mutated to 'Ala' causes steric hindrance in the core of the protein, while mutation to 'Ile' does not.", "Cys mutated to 'Ile' causes steric hindrance in the core of the protein, while mutation to 'Ala' does not."],
  E("Cys, Ala and Ile are all uncharged, so charge cannot explain the result. The residue is buried, so the protein must accommodate the new side chain in a tightly packed core. Ala (~89 Å³) is smaller than Cys (~109 Å³) and leaves only a small, tolerated cavity. Ile (~167 Å³, β-branched) is much bulkier and clashes with neighbouring residues, destabilising the fold so that the protein misfolds and aggregates.",
    {"1": "Ile is neutral; the net charge does not change.", "2": "Ala is neutral; the net charge does not change.", "3": "Ala is smaller than Cys, so it cannot cause steric clash."})),
# C4
Q("C", 2, [P("Given below are a few statements about intracellular protein transport."),
  P("A. Proteins that are destined for the lysosome are tagged with a mannose-6-phosphate (M6P) group in the Golgi apparatus, which is recognized by the M6P receptor in the trans-Golgi network.<br>B. Signal recognition particle directly mediates the insertion of proteins into the mitochondrial membrane.<br>C. The KDEL receptor in the ER and Golgi apparatus works by retrieving soluble ER resident proteins that have accidentally moved to the Golgi.<br>D. Cargo proteins that need to be exported from the ER are packaged into COPII vesicles based on the presence of an ER export signal in their cytosolic tail.<br>E. Clathrin-coated vesicles are primarily involved in vesicle trafficking between the Golgi apparatus and the ER."),
  P("Choose the option that has all correct statements.")],
  ["A, C and D", "A, B and E", "A and D only", "C and E"],
  E("A is true: GlcNAc-phosphotransferase (cis-Golgi) and uncovering enzyme generate M6P, which binds M6P receptors in the TGN. C is true: KDEL receptors bind escaped KDEL proteins in the Golgi and return them to the ER in COPI vesicles. D is true: transmembrane cargo carries cytosolic export motifs (e.g. diacidic DxE) recognised by the Sec24 subunit of COPII. So A, C and D are correct.",
    {"2": "B is false: SRP targets ribosomes to the ER membrane; mitochondrial proteins enter post-translationally via TOM/TIM. E is false (see below).", "3": "Omits C, which is true.", "4": "E is false: clathrin coats mediate TGN→endosome and plasma-membrane endocytic traffic; Golgi→ER retrograde traffic uses COPI."})),
