Q("C", 10, [P("The following table shows forest floor litter pool and aboveground litterfall data for three forest types."),
  TBL(["Forest type", "Forest floor litter pool (gC/m<sup>2</sup>)", "Aboveground litterfall (gC/m<sup>2</sup>/yr)"], [["A", "5000", "50"], ["B", "1500", "300"], ["C", "600", "600"]]),
  P("Based on the provided information, which one of the following options accurately identifies the various forest types?")],
  ["A- Tropical forest, B- Temperate deciduous forest, C- Temperate coniferous forest", "A- Temperate coniferous forest, B- Temperate deciduous forest, C- Tropical forest", "A- Temperate coniferous forest, B- Tropical forest, C- Temperate deciduous forest", "A- Tropical forest, B- Temperate coniferous forest, C- Temperate deciduous forest"],
  E("At steady state, pool ÷ input gives the mean residence time of litter: A = 5000/50 = 100 yr; B = 1500/300 = 5 yr; C = 600/600 = 1 yr. Decomposition is slowest in cold, acidic, lignin- and resin-rich conifer litter (thick organic horizons), intermediate in temperate deciduous forests, and fastest in warm, humid tropical forests. These also have the highest litterfall and a thin litter layer (Olson 1963, <i>Ecology</i> 44:322).",
    {"1": "Tropical forests have the fastest turnover, not the slowest.", "3": "Tropical litterfall is the highest of the three, which is C.", "4": "Reverses the tropical and coniferous extremes."})),
