Q("C", 8, [P("Quantitative Trait Loci (QTLs) can be identified using two main approaches: bi-parental matings (BPM) and Genome-Wide Association Studies (GWAS). Below are some descriptors related to these strategies:"),
  P("A. Large sample size<br>B. Small sample size<br>C. Population derived from controlled crosses<br>D. Random mating populations<br>E. Limited to two alleles per locus<br>F. Multiple alleles per locus<br>G. Linkage-based mapping<br>H. Linkage disequilibrium-based mapping"),
  P("The table below presents four incomplete statements regarding BPM and GWAS. Each statement can be completed using the above descriptors."),
  TBL(["Option", "Analysis typically conducted on (BPM / GWAS)", "Number of alleles that can be analysed (BPM / GWAS)", "Analysis relies on (BPM / GWAS)", "Sample size needed to identify loci with small effect (BPM / GWAS)"],
      [["i.", "C / D", "E / F", "G / H", "A / A"], ["ii.", "D / C", "E / F", "H / G", "A / B"], ["iii.", "C / D", "F / E", "H / G", "B / A"], ["iv.", "D / C", "F / E", "G / H", "B / B"]]),
  P("Which one of the following options correctly completes all statements?")],
  ["i", "ii", "iii", "iv"],
  E("Bi-parental mapping uses F₂, backcross or RIL populations from a controlled cross (C). Only the two parental alleles segregate (E), and QTLs are located by linkage to markers through recombination in a few meioses (G). GWAS uses diverse, historically random-mating panels (D) that segregate many alleles (F) and relies on population-level linkage disequilibrium built up over many generations (H). In both designs, power to detect small-effect loci rises with sample size, so both need large samples (A, A).",
    {"2": "Swaps the populations and the mapping principles.", "3": "Swaps allele number and mapping principle, and claims BPM needs small samples.", "4": "Swaps the population types and claims small samples suffice."})),
