Q("C", 3, [P("The mRNA of the <i>E. coli</i> lac operon contains the open reading frames for <i>lacZ</i>, <i>lacY</i>, and <i>lacA</i> genes from a single cistron. It is observed that <i>lacZ</i> is translated more frequently than <i>lacY</i> or <i>lacA</i>. Which one of the following statements best describes the reason for this observation?")],
  ["The Shine-Dalgarno sequence is present upstream of <i>lacZ</i>, but not <i>lacY</i> or <i>lacA</i>, affecting translation initiation rates.", "Inhibitory RNA structures are present upstream of the AUG codon of <i>lacY</i> and <i>lacA</i>, but not <i>lacZ</i>, affecting translation initiation rates.", "Variations in the Shine-Dalgarno sequence upstream of <i>lacZ</i>, <i>lacY</i>, and <i>lacA</i> have different affinities for the ribosome, affecting translation initiation rates.", "Inhibitory RNA structures are present in the <i>lacY</i> and <i>lacA</i> coding sequence but not <i>lacZ</i>, affecting translation elongation rates."],
  E("In a polycistronic bacterial mRNA, each ORF has its own ribosome-binding site, and the three are initiated independently. Differences in Shine–Dalgarno complementarity to the 16S rRNA 3′ end (anti-SD) and in spacing to the AUG give different initiation efficiencies. This is why the proteins are made in unequal amounts (roughly Z &gt; Y &gt; A).",
    {"1": "Every ORF needs a ribosome-binding site; lacY and lacA have their own SDs.", "2": "The key names SD-strength variation, not secondary structure, as the primary cause.", "4": "Translation rates in bacteria are controlled mainly at initiation, not elongation."})),
