Q("C", 4, [P("The following statements are made regarding apoptosis in the nematode, <i>C. elegans</i>:"),
  P("A. The human ortholog of <i>C. elegans</i> CED-9 is overexpressed in a B-cell lymphoma.<br>B. A <i>ced-9(gain-of-function); ced-3(loss-of-function)</i> double mutant will have more than 947 non-gonadal cells.<br>C. If purified EGL-1 is added to a CED-9/CED-4 complex <i>in vitro</i>, the autocleavage of CED-3 does not occur.<br>D. CED-8 is a multi-spanning plasma membrane protein that is required for externalization of phosphatidylserine."),
  P("Which one of the following options represents all correct statements?")],
  ["A, B, C and D", "A and B only", "A, B and D only", "B, C and D only"],
  E("A is true: the CED-9 ortholog BCL-2 is overexpressed by the t(14;18) translocation in follicular B-cell lymphoma; human BCL-2 can even substitute for CED-9 in worms (Vaux, Weissman &amp; Kim 1992, <i>Science</i> 258:1955). B is true: both mutations block the core death pathway, so the cells that normally die (~131 in the hermaphrodite) survive and the somatic cell count exceeds wild type. D is true: CED-8, an Xk-family multi-pass membrane protein, promotes phosphatidylserine exposure on apoptotic cells (Suzuki et al. 2013, <i>Science</i> 341:403).",
    {"1": "C is false: EGL-1 binding to CED-9 displaces CED-4 (Yan et al. 2005, <i>Nature</i> 437:831), freeing CED-4 to oligomerise into the apoptosome and drive CED-3 autoactivation.", "2": "Omits D, which is true.", "4": "Includes C and omits A."})),
# C16
Q("C", 9, [P("The following statements describe change in allele frequencies over time."),
  P("A. Fixation of an allele is purely by chance, while other alleles are lost.<br>B. Genetic drift can lead to the loss of certain alleles over time, reducing genetic diversity within the population.<br>C. Changes in allele frequencies are due to positive selection.<br>D. There is a pronounced effect in small populations, where random events can drastically alter allele frequencies."),
  P("Which one of the following options represents the combination of all correct statements if allele frequencies change purely due to genetic drift?")],
  ["A, B and C", "A, B and D", "B, C and D", "A, C and D"],
  E("Under pure drift, which allele fixes is random: a neutral allele's fixation probability equals its current frequency (A). Heterozygosity decays as H<sub>t</sub> = H<sub>0</sub>(1 − 1/2N)<sup>t</sup> (B). The variance of the change in allele frequency per generation is p(1−p)/2N, so the effect is strongest in small populations (D).",
    {"1": "C invokes selection, which is excluded by \"purely due to genetic drift\".", "3": "C is false under pure drift.", "4": "C is false under pure drift."})),
# C17
Q("C", 14, [P("The figure given below represents the same data in six different ways. \"A\" represents the scatter plot of all data points and \"B\" is its corresponding box and whisker plot. \"C\" to \"F\" represent the same dataset with different measures of central tendency alongside various measures of variation (SEM - Standard Error of Mean, SD - Standard Deviation, CI - 95% confidence interval)."),
  IMG("C17", "Panels A to F of one dataset: A scatter of about 20 points from 5 to 21; B box plot with median 10; C point at 10 with bars about 7.5 to 14; D point at 11 with the widest bars, about 6.5 to 15.5; E point at 11 with bars about 9 to 13; F point at 11 with the narrowest bars, about 10 to 12"),
  P("Which one of the following options is a correct representation of the data?")],
  ["C = Mean ± quartiles; D = Median ± SEM; E = Median ± CI", "D = Mean ± SD; E = Mean ± CI; F = Mean ± SEM", "C = Median with quartiles; D = Mean ± SEM; E = Mean with CI", "D = Mean ± SEM; E = Mean ± CI; F = Mean ± SD"],
  E("C sits at 10, the median shown in the box plot, with bars matching the box (interquartile range), so C is the median with quartiles. D, E and F share a centre of ~11 (the mean, pulled up by the right-skewed high values). For <i>n</i> ≈ 20, SEM = SD/√<i>n</i> ≈ SD/4.5 and the 95% CI ≈ mean ± 2.1·SEM ≈ ± 0.47·SD. So the widest bar is SD (D), the intermediate one is the CI (E) and the narrowest is SEM (F).",
    {"1": "C's centre is the median (10), not the mean, and D, E and F are centred on the mean.", "3": "C is right, but D (the widest bar) cannot be the SEM.", "4": "SD bars must be the widest, not the narrowest."})),
# C18
Q("C", 5, [P("The following statements were made regarding regulation of aging in <i>C. elegans</i>:"),
  P("A. In most cells, p53 remains bound to a repressor protein to keep it inactive, which is activated under oxidative stress when DNA damage separates p53 from its repressor.<br>B. DAF-2 functions as an insulin-like growth factor receptor to block Forkhead transcription factor and increases the life span.<br>C. When DAF-2 is not active, cells reduce the production of DNA repair enzymes.<br>D. Dietary restriction increases mTORC1 activity, enhancing functional stem cells and longevity."),
  P("Which one of the following options has the combination of all correct statements?")],
  ["A and B", "B and C", "C and D", "A and D"],
  E("NTA's key is A and B. A is true: p53 (CEP-1 in worms) is held inactive by a repressor (MDM2 in mammals) and released by stress-induced post-translational modification. B is the textbook description of the insulin/IGF-1 pathway: DAF-2 signalling, via AGE-1/PI3K and AKT, phosphorylates and excludes the FOXO factor DAF-16 from the nucleus. Read in the key's sense, blocking this DAF-2 → DAF-16 inhibition extends lifespan; <i>daf-2</i> mutants live about twice as long (Kenyon et al. 1993, <i>Nature</i> 366:461).",
    {"2": "C is false: with DAF-2 inactive, nuclear DAF-16 <i>up-regulates</i> stress-resistance and repair genes (SOD-3, catalases, small HSPs).", "3": "C and D are false.", "4": "D is false: dietary restriction <i>reduces</i> TOR signalling, and TOR inhibition extends lifespan."},
    ["Strictly worded, B is imprecise: active DAF-2 shortens lifespan, and its <i>loss</i> extends it. Choose A and B because C and D are unambiguously false."])),
