Q("C", 4, [P("In an experiment, FITC-CD4 and PE-CD8 were used to stain thymocytes. The cells were then run through a flow cytometer and the data were plotted as CD4 vs CD8. The results are shown in the figure below and following statements are made:"),
  IMG("C19", "Flow cytometry plot of thymocytes, CD4 versus CD8: CD4 single positive 7.06%, double positive 87.5%, double negative 3.58%, CD8 single positive 1.86%"),
  P("A. Rearrangement of TCR-β locus is initiated in cells included in the quadrant containing 3.58% of the population.<br>B. TCR-α locus rearrangement occurs in cells included in the quadrant containing 87.5% population.<br>C. FITC-CD4 and PE-CD8 cannot stain the same cells.<br>D. Rearranged TCR-γδ receptor is expressed on 7.06% population of cells."),
  P("Which one of the following options represents the combination of all correct statements?")],
  ["A and B only", "A and D", "A, B and C", "B only"],
  E("A is true: the 3.58% quadrant is CD4⁻CD8⁻ (double negative). TCRβ (and γ/δ) rearrangement starts at the DN2–DN3 stage, and β-selection drives progression. B is true: the 87.5% quadrant is CD4⁺CD8⁺ (double positive), where RAG is re-expressed and TCRα rearranges (with repeated attempts) before positive selection.",
    {"2": "D is false: the 7.06% quadrant is CD4 single positive (αβ T cells); most γδ T cells are CD4⁻CD8⁻.", "3": "C is false: the DP cells (87.5%) are stained by both antibodies at once.", "4": "Omits A, which is true."})),
# C20
Q("C", 7, [P("Men suffering from enlarged prostate disease were prescribed drugs that would specifically target the androgen receptor (AR). While developing the drug, the following considerations were deliberated on:"),
  P("A. Drugs should target the N-terminal domain of the AR.<br>B. Drugs should not target the NLS domain of the AR.<br>C. The drug should bind to the ligand-binding domain of the AR.<br>D. The drug should activate CYP17A1 to facilitate conversion of pregnenolone to DHEA."),
  P("Which one of the following combinations of considerations will develop the best drug for treatment of enlarged prostate?")],
  ["A and B", "B and C", "C and D", "A and C"],
  E("Two validated ways to block AR exist. (C) Ligand-binding-domain antagonists compete with DHT; examples are flutamide, bicalutamide and enzalutamide (the latter also impairs nuclear translocation and DNA binding). (A) N-terminal-domain inhibitors (e.g. EPI-001 or ralaniten) block the AF-1 transactivation function, which carries most AR activity and remains active in LBD-truncated splice variants such as AR-V7.",
    {"1": "B is wrong: blocking nuclear import (the NLS/hinge) is a legitimate way to inhibit AR, so there is no reason to avoid it.", "2": "B is wrong (see option 1).", "3": "D is counterproductive: CYP17A1 drives androgen synthesis, and therapy <i>inhibits</i> it (abiraterone)."})),
# C21
Q("C", 3, [P("The helicase activity of <i>E. coli</i> DnaB was investigated using the following two substrates (I and II) under various conditions, followed by gel electrophoresis and autoradiography. The results of these experiments are depicted below:"),
  IMG("C21", "Autoradiograph. Substrate I (fully duplex, one strand radiolabelled): lane with SSB, DnaB and ATP stays duplex; boiled lane gives single strand; untreated lane duplex. Substrate II (forked duplex with single-stranded 5' and 3' tails): SSB+DnaB+ATP gives single strand; SSB+DnaB without ATP stays mostly duplex; SSB+ATP without DnaB gives faint duplex and faint single strand; boiled gives single strand; untreated duplex"),
  P("The following statements are made purely from the results shown above:"),
  P("A. DnaB can unwind only a partially unwound DNA.<br>B. SSB inhibits the unwinding activity of DnaB.<br>C. DnaB unwinds DNA in the 5′ to 3′ direction.<br>D. DnaB requires ATP for DNA unwinding."),
  P("Which one of the following options represents the combination of all correct statements?")],
  ["A, B and D", "A, C and D", "C and D only", "A and D only"],
  E("A is supported: the fully duplex, blunt substrate I is not unwound even with SSB, DnaB and ATP, whereas forked substrate II is fully converted to single strands. DnaB needs a single-stranded loading region. D is supported: on substrate II, omitting ATP leaves the duplex intact.",
    {"1": "B is not supported: SSB is present in the lane where unwinding is complete.", "2": "C cannot be inferred: substrate II has both 5′ and 3′ single-stranded tails, so polarity cannot be told apart. (DnaB is in fact a 5′→3′ helicase, but that is not shown by this data.)", "3": "C is not shown, and A is omitted."})),
