Q("C", 9, [P("Two closely related sympatric ladybird beetle species in a rainforest have evolved to specialise on different insect prey. Which one of the following statements does NOT explain the speciation process in these beetle species?")],
  ["Populations exploited different diets in the rainforests.", "Over time, natural selection favoured traits that allowed the consumption of distinct diets.", "Diverging populations developed differences in diet. However, it did not lead to reproductive isolation.", "Temporal differentiation in their foraging activity led to their distinct diets."],
  E("Speciation requires reproductive isolation. Ecological divergence in diet that does <i>not</i> produce reproductive isolation leaves a single interbreeding species with polymorphic feeding, so it cannot explain the origin of two species.",
    {"1": "Divergent resource use is the starting point of ecological (sympatric) speciation.", "2": "Divergent natural selection on feeding traits is the mechanism of ecological speciation.", "4": "Temporal (allochronic) differentiation can both separate diets and reduce encounters between populations, contributing to isolation."})),
# C23
Q("C", 8, [P("Given below are a few statements regarding gene actions observed in plants."),
  P("A. In terms of pollination, self-pollinated species often exhibit additive gene action.<br>B. Non-additive gene action is less prevalent in cross-pollinated species.<br>C. Simply inherited (qualitative, oligogenic) traits predominantly exhibit non-additive and epistatic gene action.<br>D. Genetic fixation of superior genes will be more difficult with dominance gene action."),
  P("Which one of the following options represents the combination of all correct statements?")],
  ["A, B and C", "A, C and D", "B, C and D", "A and B only"],
  E("A is true: inbreeding fixes homozygotes, so the exploitable variance in self-pollinated crops is mainly additive (and additive × additive), which suits pure-line and pedigree selection. C is true: oligogenic qualitative traits usually show complete dominance and classical epistatic ratios, i.e. non-additive action. D is true: dominance (and overdominance) effects come from heterozygote interactions that break down on selfing, so they cannot be fixed in a true-breeding line; only additive effects can.",
    {"1": "B is false: cross-pollinated species retain heterozygosity, and non-additive (dominance) variance is more prevalent. This is the basis of heterosis and hybrid breeding in maize.", "3": "B is false.", "4": "B is false, and C and D are omitted."})),
