Q("C", 3, [P("Given below are different types of genetic manipulations of <i>E. coli trp</i> operon (Column X) and their consequences on its transcription (Column Y) under high tryptophan concentration."),
  IMG("C28", "trp operon map: trpR, promoter P, operator O, leader trpL containing sequences 1, 2, 3 and 4 with the attenuator, followed by trpE, trpD, trpC, trpB and trpA"),
  MATCH([["A. Inserting bases between the leader peptide gene and sequence 2", "i. Complete attenuation"], ["B. Inserting bases between sequences 2 and 3", "ii. Less attenuation"], ["C. Deleting sequence 4", "iii. More attenuation"], ["D. Elimination of ribosome-binding site for the leader peptide", "iv. No attenuation"]]),
  P("Which one of the following options represents all correct matches between Column X and Column Y?")],
  ["A (i) B (ii) C (iii) D (iv)", "A (i) B (iii) C (ii) D (iv)", "A (iv) B (ii) C (iii) D (i)", "A (ii) B (iii) C (iv) D (i)"],
  E("At high Trp, the ribosome translates the leader peptide (whose Trp codons lie in region 1) and stops at its stop codon, covering regions 1–2. Region 3 then pairs with 4 to form the terminator (attenuation). (A) Spacing the leader ORF further from region 2 means the terminating ribosome no longer covers region 2, so 2:3 (antiterminator) can form part of the time: <b>less</b> attenuation (ii). (B) Lengthening the 2–3 spacer weakens 2:3 pairing and favours 3:4: <b>more</b> attenuation (iii). (C) Without region 4 no terminator can form: <b>no</b> attenuation (iv). (D) With no leader translation, 1:2 and then 3:4 form by default, terminating transcription constitutively: <b>complete</b> attenuation (i) (Yanofsky 1981, <i>Nature</i> 289:751).",
    {"1": "Reverses the consequences of C and D.", "2": "Deleting the terminator half cannot increase attenuation.", "3": "An untranslated leader gives termination, not read-through."})),
