Q("C", 14, [P("Two newly identified proteins, X and Y, are tested for sequence-specific DNA binding activity. The results of an electrophoretic mobility shift assay (EMSA) with a labeled DNA fragment and proteins X and Y in various combinations are shown below."),
  IMG("C31", "EMSA. Lanes: 1 DNA only (free DNA); 2 DNA + X (shifted band); 3 DNA + Y (shifted band slightly lower); 4 DNA + X + Y (two shifted bands plus a higher supershifted band); 5 DNA + X + poly dI:dC (free DNA only); 6 DNA + Y + poly dI:dC (shifted band retained)"),
  P("Poly dI:dC is a DNA duplex of polyinosine and polycytosine. Which one of the following options represents the correct interpretation of the results obtained?")],
  ["Both X and Y are sequence-specific DNA binding proteins.", "X does not bind to DNA and Y binds to specific sequence.", "Both proteins bind to DNA but Y binds in a sequence-specific manner.", "X competes with Y to bind the same sequence."],
  E("Both proteins shift the probe on their own (lanes 2 and 3), so both bind DNA. Poly dI:dC is a non-specific competitor: it soaks up proteins that bind any DNA, but not those that recognise a specific sequence. The X shift disappears with poly dI:dC (lane 5), so X binds non-specifically. The Y shift persists (lane 6), so Y binds a specific sequence.",
    {"1": "X loses binding with poly dI:dC, so it is not sequence-specific.", "2": "X clearly shifts the probe in lane 2.", "4": "With both proteins a slower ternary complex appears (lane 4): they can bind together, not competitively."})),
# C32
Q("C", 10, [P("The following statements are about parental care and variance in reproductive success in a bird species."),
  P("A. If females provide more parental care than males, the variance in male reproductive success is significantly greater than that of females.<br>B. Where only males provide parental care, the variance in female reproductive success is significantly higher than that of males.<br>C. In the case of biparental care, the variance in male reproductive success is significantly greater than that of females.<br>D. In the case of biparental care, the variance in female reproductive success is significantly greater than that of males."),
  P("Select the option that identifies the combination of all correct statements.")],
  ["A and B", "A and C", "B and D", "C and D"],
  E("Under Trivers' (1972) parental-investment theory, the sex that invests less competes for the sex that invests more and so shows the higher variance in reproductive success (Bateman's principle). A is true: with female-biased care, male RS variance is greater. B is true: with male-only care (sex-role reversal, e.g. jacanas and phalaropes), females compete and have the higher variance.",
    {"2": "C is false: with roughly equal biparental care, neither sex shows significantly greater variance (most socially monogamous birds).", "3": "D is false for the same reason.", "4": "Both C and D are false."})),
