Q("C", 1, [P("Three strands of a beta-sheet of 4 peptides are hydrogen bonded with the orientation of the strands (parallel or antiparallel denoted by the arrowheads) as shown in the figure. Each strand consists of identical residues, where N and C represent terminal residues of the peptide. The three strands of each peptide are linked by amino acid sequences of the smallest length possible."),
  IMG("C36", "Four three-stranded sheets. Peptide 1: down, up, down (all antiparallel). Peptide 2: down, down, down (all parallel). Peptide 3: down, down, up (parallel pair then antiparallel). Peptide 4: down, up, up (antiparallel pair then parallel)"),
  P("Which one of the following options is correct regarding the length of the peptides 1 to 4?")],
  ["(Peptide 1 = Peptide 2) &lt; Peptide 3 &lt; Peptide 4", "Peptide 1 &lt; (Peptide 3 = Peptide 4) &lt; Peptide 2", "Peptide 1 &lt; Peptide 3 &lt; Peptide 4 &lt; Peptide 2", "Peptide 1 &lt; Peptide 2 &lt; Peptide 3 &lt; Peptide 4"],
  E("Adjacent antiparallel strands can be joined by a tight β-hairpin (a 2–5-residue turn). Adjacent parallel strands end and start at opposite edges of the sheet, so the connection must cross from one end of the sheet to the other: a crossover connection, typically a long loop or a β-α-β unit. The strands are identical, so length depends only on the connections. Peptide 1 has 2 hairpins (shortest). Peptides 3 and 4 each have 1 hairpin + 1 crossover (equal). Peptide 2 has 2 crossovers (longest).",
    {"1": "Peptide 2 needs two long crossovers, so it cannot equal peptide 1.", "3": "Peptides 3 and 4 have the same set of connections, so they are equal in length.", "4": "Peptide 2 is the longest, not the second shortest."})),
