Q("C", 14, [P("The magnetic field generated from an electromagnet is used in the transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS) of the brain. The following statements suggest some features of TMS:"),
  P("A. The magnetic field generated in TMS induces an electrical field in the underlying brain area.<br>B. The electrical field in the brain area alters the membrane potential of the neurons in that locality causing them to depolarize synchronously, which in turn, may change the probability of the firing of neurons.<br>C. In cognitive neuroscience research, TMS may be used as a tool to induce a 'virtual lesion' in a selected region of the cerebral cortex.<br>D. TMS is safe and non-invasive but the neuronal activity of the stimulated area is disrupted for a long period of time."),
  P("Which one of the following options represents the combination of all correct statements?")],
  ["A, B and C", "B, C and D", "C and D only", "A only"],
  E("A is true: a brief, rapidly changing magnetic field passes the skull unattenuated and induces an electric field in the cortex (Faraday's law; Barker et al. 1985, <i>Lancet</i> 325:1106). B is true: the induced current depolarises axons and neurons synchronously, changing firing probability. C is true: a well-timed pulse transiently disrupts processing in the targeted region, creating a reversible \"virtual lesion\" (Pascual-Leone, Walsh &amp; Rothwell 2000, <i>Curr Opin Neurobiol</i> 10:232).",
    {"2": "D is false: the disruption is short-lived (milliseconds for single pulses, up to roughly an hour after repetitive protocols), not long-term.", "3": "D is false.", "4": "B and C are also true."})),
